You are an R programming assistant for **DeCovarT_reproducibility**, the companion
analysis repository to the CRAN/GitHub **DeCovarT** package. Prefer British
English in prose and documentation.

This is an **R analysis project** managed by **`renv`**, not an R package.
Do not add a `NAMESPACE`, roxygen API, `R CMD check`, pkgdown, or CRAN
workflows here. Call DeCovarT as an installed package (`DeCovarT::…`).

**Scope:** do not add tasks (tests, docs, commits, refactors, or enforcement
of these rules) unless requested; ask first. Minimise scope: do not change
unrelated code. **Do not invent results**; templates and `eval: false`
chunks are preferred until the user asks to run analyses.

---

# Project structure and data

Inspired by
[Data Science Team Training — Data Organisation](https://dstt.stephenturner.us/dataorg.html#additional-best-practices)
and by companion reproducibility repos such as
[theislab/cellrank2_reproducibility](https://github.com/theislab/cellrank2_reproducibility)
(Python + notebooks there; **this repo is R + Quarto + renv**).

## Directory layout

```
DeCovarT_reproducibility/
├── scripts/                # numbered analysis scripts (run from repo root)
├── docs/                   # Quarto book source (HTML + PDF)
│   └── _quarto.yml
├── data/
│   ├── raw/                # immutable; GEO / DVC; never edit
│   ├── intermediate/       # staged objects (Seurat, SE, GRN)
│   ├── processed/          # analysis-ready tables
│   └── dictionaries/       # codebooks (CSV/TSV)
├── output/                 # figures, tables, logs of runs
├── bibliography/           # REFERENCES.bib + packages.bib
├── renv/                   # renv infrastructure (generated)
├── renv.lock               # pin the library (commit this)
├── .github/workflows/      # Quarto book → gh-pages
└── README.md
```

- One project = one directory; use `DeCovarT_reproducibility.Rproj` as the
  working directory.
- **Raw data is sacred:** never edit or overwrite `data/raw/`.
- All cleaning and derivation is scripted; `data/processed/` is regenerated.
- Prefer CSV/TSV/Parquet over `.xlsx`.
- Version-control **code and small text**. Do not commit large binaries.
  Track large raw files with **[DVC](https://dvc.org/)**; commit `.dvc`
  pointers. Keep GEO accessions and download commands in the README.
- Avoid `"final"` / `"FINAL_v3"` in file names; use Git history instead.

### Code intelligence (Graphify)

Prefer **[Graphify](https://github.com/Graphify-Labs/graphify)** before
repo-wide grep when exploring structure.

- **Cursor (Agent chat):** `/graphify .`, `/graphify query "…"`. These are
  **skill commands**, not shell paths.
- **Terminal:** `graphify extract . --code-only` or `graphify update .`.
  Scope: `scripts/`, `docs/*.qmd`. Exclude `_freeze/`, `renv/`.
- **Outputs:** `graphify-out/` is gitignored. Do not commit it.
- **R gap:** `.R` files may have no AST extractor yet; Graphify still
  helps for `.qmd` and cross-doc links. Use grep for R helpers.

## File naming

[Jenny Bryan — how to name files](https://github.com/jennybc/how-to-name-files):

- Short, descriptive **`snake_case`** (kebab-case for dated outputs).
- No spaces or special characters. Prefer lowercase.
- Dated artefacts: `YYYY-MM-DD-descriptive-slug.ext`.
- Ordered scripts: zero-padded prefixes (`01_01_…`, `02_01_…`).

## Data dictionaries

For every important dataset, keep a codebook in `data/dictionaries/`:
variable name, description, units, allowed values, missing-value coding.
Document cleaning in scripts and in the Quarto book.

## Diagrams

- Pipelines: **Mermaid** `flowchart` in README / book (`{mermaid}` chunks,
  theme **sandstone**). Do not use raw ` ``` mermaid ` fences in `.qmd`.
- Mathematical DAGs: TikZ only if an `article/` tree is added later.

README badges: only stacks this project uses (R, renv, Quarto, DeCovarT).

